<b>ט.</b> "The Akedah section" - before sacrificial sections. One may say Akedah and manna even on Shabbat. Rabbeinu Bachya writes (Tzav) that recitation without understanding is insufficient; one must grasp the section and God's wonders, as with saying Ashrei three times daily.<br>
<br>
<b>י.</b> "And shelamim" - after olah and shelamim one says the libations section, for no sacrifice is without libations; chatat and asham are exceptions. Magen Avraham asks why Beit Yosef lists olah before chatat despite the rule that chatat precedes; he resolves that if one knows he is liable for chatat, he should say chatat first. Other poskim imply olah is always recited first in this context.<br>
<br>
<b>יא.</b> "In public" - due to heretics saying only these are Torah, especially today; thus they are not printed in public-only booklets. Some (Maharsha) permit public recitation before Barukh She'amar, prohibiting only fixed insertion among berakhot like Shema. Our practice is to forbid public recitation altogether; for individuals, before or after prayer is allowed, but fixing it inside the blessings is forbidden (see Shvut Yaakov).
